WEBVTT

00:00.210 --> 00:02.550
Instructor: Okay, so onto another use

00:02.550 --> 00:04.320
for the checkout command.

00:04.320 --> 00:07.920
We can use git checkout to discard changes from a file

00:07.920 --> 00:09.420
basically to revert back

00:09.420 --> 00:12.210
to whenever we previously committed.

00:12.210 --> 00:13.760
So I'm going to demonstrate this

00:13.760 --> 00:15.120
in the next couple of commands.

00:15.120 --> 00:19.620
There's restore is another command on a very, very boring

00:19.620 --> 00:22.080
stupid repository that will make it very

00:22.080 --> 00:23.910
clear how this works.

00:23.910 --> 00:26.520
So I'm gonna just make, I already have a repo actually

00:26.520 --> 00:28.590
or a new folder called undoing stuff.

00:28.590 --> 00:29.430
It's not a repo.

00:29.430 --> 00:32.910
I'm going to init and then I'm gonna make two files.

00:32.910 --> 00:37.910
And again, very silly one will be called, how about cat.txt

00:40.440 --> 00:42.543
and the other will be dog.txt.

00:43.470 --> 00:47.517
Okay, so two files and then I'm gonna add my first commit.

00:47.517 --> 00:52.517
Git status, okay, get commit, add cat and dog files.

00:54.720 --> 00:58.230
Then in both of them, gonna open them up over here,

00:58.230 --> 01:02.580
I'm just gonna write first commit and make a commit.

01:02.580 --> 01:05.010
And then I'm gonna do second commit and make a commit.

01:05.010 --> 01:07.290
Basically I have a record in two files.

01:07.290 --> 01:08.850
Every commit I make, I'm just gonna say

01:08.850 --> 01:11.340
first commit, second commit, third commit, and so on.

01:11.340 --> 01:13.290
And this will make it easy for me to demonstrate

01:13.290 --> 01:16.080
when I go back in time, oh we're back to first commit,

01:16.080 --> 01:17.520
or second, or third.

01:17.520 --> 01:19.230
So let's do that here.

01:19.230 --> 01:24.230
Get status, get add dot, get commit first commit.

01:24.840 --> 01:25.950
Technically it's our second

01:25.950 --> 01:28.620
but I'll call it first commit

01:28.620 --> 01:30.570
and then I'll do the same thing.

01:30.570 --> 01:32.163
Second, commit.

01:33.900 --> 01:35.370
Okay.

01:35.370 --> 01:40.370
Git dot git commit dash m second commit.

01:40.920 --> 01:42.600
And I'll do it one more time.

01:42.600 --> 01:44.253
Third commit.

01:45.540 --> 01:46.893
Third commit.

01:48.120 --> 01:53.120
Git add dot get commit dash m third commit.

01:53.520 --> 01:57.060
Okay, very boring, like I said, and trivial stupid.

01:57.060 --> 02:00.510
But we have two files, both of them have three lines.

02:00.510 --> 02:02.970
We added one line at a time in a commit.

02:02.970 --> 02:06.990
Now what I wanna show is that we can use git checkout

02:06.990 --> 02:10.470
with HEAD and then a file name or multiple file names

02:10.470 --> 02:13.320
to revert changes or to discard changes that we've

02:13.320 --> 02:16.080
made back to whenever we last committed.

02:16.080 --> 02:19.770
So let's say that, you know, I'm working

02:19.770 --> 02:24.770
and I accidentally type a bunch of stuff in here and I save

02:25.050 --> 02:28.380
and type a bunch of stuff in this file too and I save.

02:28.380 --> 02:31.440
And of course over here git status

02:31.440 --> 02:34.110
we see, oh, I've modified those two files,

02:34.110 --> 02:37.290
if I don't want to commit that, if that's a mistake

02:37.290 --> 02:40.440
one option is just to manually delete that, right?

02:40.440 --> 02:44.130
Delete that, delete this, that's fine.

02:44.130 --> 02:46.410
But if we have a ton of files where I've done work

02:46.410 --> 02:48.720
and I just want to like get rid of all the changes

02:48.720 --> 02:52.500
I've done, I can do it using git checkout HEAD.

02:52.500 --> 02:55.650
So again, silly, but this is a demonstration of it.

02:55.650 --> 02:58.290
So I have this extra stuff in both of them.

02:58.290 --> 03:02.040
The last commit I made stopped right there and right here.

03:02.040 --> 03:07.040
So if I do git checkout HEAD

03:07.350 --> 03:11.670
and then a file name, how about dog, I'm telling git

03:11.670 --> 03:16.560
take whatever the contents of dog we're at HEAD, right

03:16.560 --> 03:19.770
'cause HEAD is different from where I am right now.

03:19.770 --> 03:21.630
HEAD is pointing at that last commit

03:21.630 --> 03:23.460
well it's pointing at master, which is pointing

03:23.460 --> 03:26.070
at the last commit on the master branch.

03:26.070 --> 03:29.532
So HEAD is still referencing this

03:29.532 --> 03:31.332
and this new stuff is gonna go away.

03:32.850 --> 03:34.320
And there it's gone.

03:34.320 --> 03:37.200
Now Cat.txt still has that stuff

03:37.200 --> 03:39.450
but if I do get checkout HEAD

03:39.450 --> 03:44.450
and then Cat.txt, that's gone as well, right?

03:45.000 --> 03:46.170
Okay.

03:46.170 --> 03:47.430
So that's one way of doing it.

03:47.430 --> 03:49.590
Another option is to use the shorter version

03:49.590 --> 03:52.500
git checkout dash, dash instead of typing HEAD

03:52.500 --> 03:56.670
and then the file or files we want to revert back to HEAD.

03:56.670 --> 03:58.480
So if I do a bunch of work again

04:00.060 --> 04:05.060
in this file and in dog, I could even get rid of that,

04:05.130 --> 04:07.680
get rid of that, just screw everything up

04:07.680 --> 04:10.740
and then I'm realize, oh crap, this is a disaster.

04:10.740 --> 04:13.050
I wanna undo those changes but I can't.

04:13.050 --> 04:15.568
Or maybe I mean I could just do Command Z,

04:15.568 --> 04:17.220
but let's say I can't,

04:17.220 --> 04:21.090
over here I could do git checkout HEAD

04:21.090 --> 04:23.790
or just git checkout dash dash cat.

04:23.790 --> 04:25.353
I'll do both of them and dog.

04:27.120 --> 04:29.700
And we're back to where we were at HEAD

04:29.700 --> 04:32.670
where HEAD is still referring to this commit.

04:32.670 --> 04:34.800
HEAD is always, unless we switch branches

04:34.800 --> 04:36.270
it's going to point at this master branch

04:36.270 --> 04:37.680
and that master branch doesn't move

04:37.680 --> 04:39.180
until we make a new commit.

04:39.180 --> 04:42.900
Git log, and this is where HEAD still is.

04:42.900 --> 04:45.990
All right, so to recap that git checkout HEAD

04:45.990 --> 04:48.480
and then a file name or multiple file names

04:48.480 --> 04:52.020
we'll reset those files contents back to whatever it looked

04:52.020 --> 04:54.990
like in your last commit or when you last committed,

04:54.990 --> 04:58.290
AKA, where HEAD is still referencing.

04:58.290 --> 05:01.320
And then there's a shorter version, git checkout dash dash

05:01.320 --> 05:02.820
does the same thing.

05:02.820 --> 05:06.750
What's extra confusing though, or kind of

05:06.750 --> 05:08.820
annoying to have to teach you is that there's

05:08.820 --> 05:12.570
a whole new command that also does the same thing and more.

05:12.570 --> 05:14.610
And that's what we're gonna learn in the next video.

05:14.610 --> 05:16.173
It's called git restore.
